# deploy/compose/Caddyfile.bootstrap
#
# The real Caddyfile for the compose blue/green stack. Installed
# unconditionally by configure.sh at /home/puni1/wbs/caddy/Caddyfile on every run —
# safe to overwrite every time, since this file's own content never changes;
# only the `site.caddy` it imports (rendered per-deploy by
# tools/tool-remote-scripts' swap executor) does.
#
# Previously this file was a *temporary* placeholder, installed ONLY if
# Caddyfile didn't already exist, with the real `import site.caddy` version
# left for "the deploy pipeline" to write later once a real release
# existed — except nothing ever did that. Task 12's rehearsal caught the
# result live: `caddy reload` kept exiting 0 while Caddy silently kept
# serving the old placeholder forever, because `reload` only re-applies
# whatever Caddyfile already says, and the placeholder never mentioned
# site.caddy at all. The swap looked like it succeeded on every run; routing
# never actually changed.
#
# Importing unconditionally from the start removes that two-phase lifecycle
# entirely — there is no "before a real release exists" state left to
# special-case, because `site.caddy` itself now always exists: configure.sh
# seeds it once (see the seed literal in configure.sh) with an honest
# "not yet deployed" response for every tier (same shape a real
# render-route would produce for a tier with no observed colour — see
# tools/tool-remote-scripts/src/lib/site.ts's `routeBlock`), and every real
# swap's render-route step overwrites it for real from then on.
# `log-redact.caddy` FIRST: it defines the `(access-log)` snippet that every
# site file — rendered or hand-maintained — imports. Caddy resolves imports
# in file order, so a site file that imports `access-log` before the snippet
# is defined fails the config load. That failure is deliberate and correct:
# the alternative, a vhost quietly logging unfiltered query strings, is the
# leak TASK-159 closed and TASK-160 kept closed across swaps.
#
# configure.sh does NOT clobber other `import` lines it finds here — this
# host also serves hand-added vhosts (registry, monitoring, dev, studio)
# whose imports live in the same file.
import log-redact.caddy
import site.caddy
